00:09:40.020 --> 00:09:45.150
To protect non-Azure resources,
Azure Arc plays a central role.

00:09:45.150 --> 00:09:48.660
Azure Arc simplifies
governance and management

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:51.840
by delivering a
consistent multi-cloud

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:54.780
and on-premise
management platform.

00:09:54.780 --> 00:10:01.220
In other words, you can project
servers and Kubernetes clusters

00:10:01.220 --> 00:10:05.720
that runs outside Azure
into Azure management

00:10:05.720 --> 00:10:08.870
and then apply
policies, monitoring,

00:10:08.870 --> 00:10:12.680
and security control,
including Defender for Cloud,

00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:18.060
more consistently across
all your environments.

00:10:18.060 --> 00:10:21.480
When connecting individual
non-Azure machines,

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:24.720
there's a few key
paths to understand.

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:26.880
With Azure Arc enabled,

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:30.000
you install the
Azure-connected machine agent

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:35.160
on a non-Azure host to bring
them under Azure Arc management.

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:39.870
From there, you use a Sata
Collection Rule, or DCR,

00:10:39.870 --> 00:10:41.460
to onboard monitoring

00:10:41.460 --> 00:10:44.340
by installing the Azure
Monitoring Agent,

00:10:44.340 --> 00:10:46.380
often called AMA.

00:10:46.380 --> 00:10:49.710
You can add an Azure-connected
Windows machine

00:10:49.710 --> 00:10:54.150
to a DCR resource
to install AMA.

00:10:54.150 --> 00:10:57.690
And you can also add our
connected Linux machine

00:10:57.690 --> 00:11:02.070
as a DCR resource
to install AMA.

00:11:02.070 --> 00:11:07.590
Another important integration is
Microsoft Defender for Endpoint.

00:11:07.590 --> 00:11:12.870
You can onboard at the tenant
level using direct onboarding

00:11:12.870 --> 00:11:16.650
enabled in the Defender for
Cloud management settings,

00:11:16.650 --> 00:11:20.070
and then use the standard
Microsoft Defender

00:11:20.070 --> 00:11:23.190
for Endpoint onboarding method.

00:11:23.190 --> 00:11:26.190
You can also use Azure
Portal-based onboarding

00:11:26.190 --> 00:11:29.880
approach, such as
workspace-level direct

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:33.450
onboarding, depending on
how you manage monitoring

00:11:33.450 --> 00:11:36.520
and security operations today.

00:11:37.860 --> 00:11:40.770
Let's take a look
at connecting AWS.

00:11:40.770 --> 00:11:45.330
When you onboard your AWS
account in Microsoft Defender

00:11:45.330 --> 00:11:49.650
for Cloud, you integrate
AWS Security Hub.

00:11:49.650 --> 00:11:55.080
The result is that Defender for
Cloud can now provide visibility

00:11:55.080 --> 00:11:58.980
and protection signals
across both environments

00:11:58.980 --> 00:12:03.420
so you can access posture
and surface findings

00:12:03.420 --> 00:12:06.630
into a more unified
way instead of jumping

00:12:06.630 --> 00:12:11.800
between separate
portals and dashboards.

00:12:13.320 --> 00:12:16.590
And, for Google Cloud,
the approach is similar.

00:12:16.590 --> 00:12:20.640
Onboarding your GCP account
into Microsoft Defender

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:25.080
for Cloud integrates GCP
security command center,

00:12:25.080 --> 00:12:29.040
allowing Defender for Cloud
to operate visibility

00:12:29.040 --> 00:12:33.520
and protection across
both cloud environments.

00:12:36.090 --> 00:12:38.100
Once your assets are connected,

00:12:38.100 --> 00:12:40.770
the next priority
is understanding

00:12:40.770 --> 00:12:44.910
and improving your
overall security posture.

00:12:44.910 --> 00:12:49.110
Let's explore the Cloud Security
Posture Management capabilities

00:12:49.110 --> 00:12:53.940
in Microsoft Defender for Cloud,
including scoring, benchmark,

00:12:53.940 --> 00:12:57.180
recommendation,
and risk analysis

00:12:57.180 --> 00:12:59.640
across your environment.

00:12:59.640 --> 00:13:03.120
In this module, we're
aiming for three outcomes.

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:05.940
First, you will be able
to describe the Defender

00:13:05.940 --> 00:13:08.250
for Cloud Secure Score.

00:13:08.250 --> 00:13:10.950
Second, you will
understand how Defender

00:13:10.950 --> 00:13:14.370
for Cloud implements the
Microsoft Cloud Security

00:13:14.370 --> 00:13:17.580
Benchmark, or MCSB.

00:13:17.580 --> 00:13:19.560
And, third, you will be able

00:13:19.560 --> 00:13:22.680
to explain the posture
management protection

00:13:22.680 --> 00:13:26.670
that Defender for Cloud applies
to help you reduce risk

00:13:26.670 --> 00:13:29.350
across your resources.

00:13:30.510 --> 00:13:33.360
Secure Score gives
you a measurable way

00:13:33.360 --> 00:13:37.140
to understand your
current security posture

00:13:37.140 --> 00:13:39.960
and track improvement over time.

00:13:39.960 --> 00:13:44.040
Here, we are looking at the
Security Score experience

00:13:44.040 --> 00:13:45.720
in Defender for Cloud

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:49.620
where posture improvement
actions are translated

00:13:49.620 --> 00:13:51.090
into points.

00:13:51.090 --> 00:13:54.840
The practical value
is prioritization.

00:13:54.840 --> 00:13:59.640
You can identify high-impact
recommendations,

00:13:59.640 --> 00:14:04.770
understand what's driving the
score up or down, and then use

00:14:04.770 --> 00:14:08.820
that score as a simple
consistent indicator

00:14:08.820 --> 00:14:10.860
of posture improvement

00:14:10.860 --> 00:14:15.100
across your connected
subscription and environment.

00:14:17.220 --> 00:14:21.240
It's also helpful to compare
how Secure Score is used

00:14:21.240 --> 00:14:25.080
across different Microsoft
and cloud services.

00:14:25.080 --> 00:14:28.050
For a SaaS like Microsoft 365,

00:14:28.050 --> 00:14:32.640
the scoring experience is
Microsoft's Secure Score focus

00:14:32.640 --> 00:14:37.230
on areas like identities,
devices, and apps.

00:14:37.230 --> 00:14:42.120
And it's managed through the
Microsoft 365 Security Center.

00:14:42.120 --> 00:14:45.090
For PaaS and IS scenarios,

00:14:45.090 --> 00:14:48.030
Defender for Cloud
provides a Secure Score

00:14:48.030 --> 00:14:50.820
through the Defender
for Cloud dashboard.

00:14:50.820 --> 00:14:53.760
And, when you connect
other cloud providers,

00:14:53.760 --> 00:14:58.410
you will see the scoring maps
to those ecosystems as well.

00:14:58.410 --> 00:15:01.800
For AWS, posture
findings are provided

00:15:01.800 --> 00:15:06.030
through AWS Security
Hub and, for GCP,

00:15:06.030 --> 00:15:10.260
through the GCP Security
Command Center, while Defender

00:15:10.260 --> 00:15:13.200
for Cloud brings those
signals together

00:15:13.200 --> 00:15:15.990
so you can manage
them centrally.

00:15:15.990 --> 00:15:19.830
This comparison is also
a useful optional moment

00:15:19.830 --> 00:15:21.780
to connect what you
may have learned

00:15:21.780 --> 00:15:25.710
about Microsoft Secure
Score earlier in the course

00:15:25.710 --> 00:15:30.510
with Defender for Cloud
Secure Score here.

00:15:30.510 --> 00:15:31.800
Recommendations are

00:15:31.800 --> 00:15:35.370
where posture management
becomes actionable.

00:15:35.370 --> 00:15:39.510
Here, we are looking at the
Recommendation View in Defender

00:15:39.510 --> 00:15:44.280
for Cloud which consolidates
the specific steps you can take

00:15:44.280 --> 00:15:48.390
to reduce risks, such as
hardening configuration,

00:15:48.390 --> 00:15:53.670
addressing missing controls, or
improving monitoring coverage.

00:15:53.670 --> 00:15:56.280
As you work with these
recommendations,

00:15:56.280 --> 00:16:01.380
you typically prioritize
based on severity and impact,

00:16:01.380 --> 00:16:03.960
assign ownership to
the right teams,

00:16:03.960 --> 00:16:07.110
and track remediation
progress as part

00:16:07.110 --> 00:16:10.690
of your ongoing
security operations.

00:16:11.940 --> 00:16:16.560
Defender for Cloud also helps
you align to a recognized set

00:16:16.560 --> 00:16:20.310
of security control through
the Microsoft Cloud Security

00:16:20.310 --> 00:16:23.460
Benchmark, or MCSB.

00:16:23.460 --> 00:16:28.170
This benchmark is automatically
assigned to your subscription

00:16:28.170 --> 00:16:32.160
and account when you
onboard Defender for Cloud.

00:16:32.160 --> 00:16:35.250
It builds on the cloud
security principle defined

00:16:35.250 --> 00:16:39.120
by the Azure Security
Benchmark and extends them

00:16:39.120 --> 00:16:43.950
with detailed technical
implementation guidance not only

00:16:43.950 --> 00:16:48.630
for Azure, but also for other
cloud providers like AWS

00:16:48.630 --> 00:16:53.520
and GCP, and even for
other Microsoft Cloud.

00:16:53.520 --> 00:16:57.990
The result is a consistent
control framework you can use

00:16:57.990 --> 00:17:02.340
to drive posture improvement
and compliance discussion

00:17:02.340 --> 00:17:05.340
across the environment.

00:17:05.340 --> 00:17:09.150
Building on that, you can
measure compliance directly

00:17:09.150 --> 00:17:11.010
in Defender for Cloud.

00:17:11.010 --> 00:17:16.140
The Microsoft Cloud Security
Benchmark is the default policy

00:17:16.140 --> 00:17:19.140
initiative for
Defender for Cloud,

00:17:19.140 --> 00:17:21.120
and it forms the foundation

00:17:21.120 --> 00:17:24.600
of the security
recommendations you see.

00:17:24.600 --> 00:17:27.120
This means compliance reporting

00:17:27.120 --> 00:17:30.900
and posture recommendations
are connected.

00:17:30.900 --> 00:17:34.380
As you remediate findings
and implement control,

00:17:34.380 --> 00:17:38.070
you are not only improving
your Secure Score,

00:17:38.070 --> 00:17:42.450
you are also strengthening
alignment to the benchmarks

00:17:42.450 --> 00:17:46.420
and the compliance
requirement mapped to them.

00:17:47.460 --> 00:17:49.740
Beyond scores and checklists,

00:17:49.740 --> 00:17:53.130
Defender for Cloud can
help you understand risk

00:17:53.130 --> 00:17:58.730
as an attacker would across
identities, network exposure,

00:17:58.730 --> 00:18:02.480
permissions, and
resource relationships.

00:18:02.480 --> 00:18:06.020
Here, we use Attack
Path Analysis

00:18:06.020 --> 00:18:08.750
and Cloud Security
Explorer to scan

00:18:08.750 --> 00:18:11.910
and query the Cloud
Security Graph.

00:18:11.910 --> 00:18:16.560
The Cloud Security Graph is
a graph-based context engine

00:18:16.560 --> 00:18:20.550
inside Defender for
Cloud that collects data

00:18:20.550 --> 00:18:24.600
from your multi-cloud
environment and other sources

00:18:24.600 --> 00:18:28.440
like the cloud assets
inventory, connections,

00:18:28.440 --> 00:18:32.880
and potential lateral movement
path between your resources,

00:18:32.880 --> 00:18:37.410
internet exposure, permissions,
network connection,

00:18:37.410 --> 00:18:40.740
vulnerabilities, and more.

00:18:40.740 --> 00:18:44.850
That data is used to build
a graph representing your

00:18:44.850 --> 00:18:49.260
environment which then
supports richer risk analysis

00:18:49.260 --> 00:18:51.240
than isolated alert

00:18:51.240 --> 00:18:55.630
or individual
configuration findings.

00:18:56.790 --> 00:19:02.300
Attack Path Analysis is designed
to expose the chain of condition

00:19:02.300 --> 00:19:05.360
that would enable a
successful breach.

00:19:05.360 --> 00:19:09.560
Instead of only telling you that
something is misconfigured,

00:19:09.560 --> 00:19:14.810
it shows you how multiple
weaknesses can connect turning a

00:19:14.810 --> 00:19:19.860
path from an entry point
to a high-value target.

00:19:19.860 --> 00:19:24.030
Event suggests recommendations
to remediate the issue

00:19:24.030 --> 00:19:28.680
that would break that path in
helping you prioritize fixes

00:19:28.680 --> 00:19:33.960
that reduces real-world
attack viabilities.

00:19:33.960 --> 00:19:38.370
Cloud Security Explorer gives
you a query builder experience

00:19:38.370 --> 00:19:42.690
so you can run graph-based
queries across your environment.

00:19:42.690 --> 00:19:47.550
This is especially useful when
you want to answer questions

00:19:47.550 --> 00:19:53.160
like "show me internet exposed
resources with high privileges"

00:19:53.160 --> 00:19:57.630
or "find path to sensitive
data" without having

00:19:57.630 --> 00:20:02.100
to manually correlate dozens
of separate findings.

00:20:02.100 --> 00:20:06.630
The query experience leverages
the Cloud Security Graph

00:20:06.630 --> 00:20:11.550
to surface results with context.

00:20:11.550 --> 00:20:15.510
To visualize trend and
operational insight,

00:20:15.510 --> 00:20:17.640
you can use workbooks.

00:20:17.640 --> 00:20:21.840
These give you the readymade
dashboards for scenarios

00:20:21.840 --> 00:20:26.220
like Secure Score over
time, system updates,

00:20:26.220 --> 00:20:28.620
vulnerability
assessment finding,

00:20:28.620 --> 00:20:33.030
compliance over time,
and active alerts.

00:20:33.030 --> 00:20:37.620
Workbooks help translate large
volumes of security data

00:20:37.620 --> 00:20:42.180
in a format that's easier
to track, report on,

00:20:42.180 --> 00:20:45.220
and review with stakeholders.

00:20:47.430 --> 00:20:50.310
Now that we've covered
posture management,

00:20:50.310 --> 00:20:53.910
let's move into workload
protection where Defender

00:20:53.910 --> 00:20:58.800
for Cloud shifts from how
secure are we configured

00:20:58.800 --> 00:21:03.480
to how are we detecting
and responding to threats

00:21:03.480 --> 00:21:06.510
against the workload we run.

00:21:06.510 --> 00:21:10.440
In this module, you will learn
how workloads are protected

00:21:10.440 --> 00:21:13.890
by Microsoft Defender
for Cloud, the benefits

00:21:13.890 --> 00:21:17.870
of those protections, and how
the protections function.

00:21:17.870 --> 00:21:21.950
The goal is to help you
understand not only what's

00:21:21.950 --> 00:21:26.010
covered, like servers,
containers, and storage,

00:21:26.010 --> 00:21:29.880
but also how Defender for
Cloud connects signals,

00:21:29.880 --> 00:21:36.660
generates alerts, and supports
investigation and remediation.

00:21:36.660 --> 00:21:38.970
For server protection, Defender

00:21:38.970 --> 00:21:43.290
for Cloud offers a Microsoft
Defender for Server plan.

00:21:43.290 --> 00:21:47.070
Plan 1 deploys Microsoft
Defender for Endpoint

00:21:47.070 --> 00:21:50.760
to your servers and provides
an important benefit.

00:21:50.760 --> 00:21:53.220
Licensing for Microsoft Defender

00:21:53.220 --> 00:21:58.140
for Endpoint is charged per
hour instead of per seat,

00:21:58.140 --> 00:22:02.100
which can lower cost when you
are protecting a virtual machine

00:22:02.100 --> 00:22:04.560
only while they are running.

00:22:04.560 --> 00:22:08.760
Defender for Endpoint can
also deploy automatically

00:22:08.760 --> 00:22:13.980
to cloud workload, ensuring
new workloads are protected

00:22:13.980 --> 00:22:15.810
as they spin up.

00:22:15.810 --> 00:22:19.770
And the alerts and vulnerability
data coming from Defender

00:22:19.770 --> 00:22:24.000
for Endpoints are then shown
directly in Microsoft Defender

00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:30.150
for Cloud so you can view from
a more unified security view.

00:22:30.150 --> 00:22:34.590
Plan 2, which was formerly known
simply as Defender for Servers,

00:22:34.590 --> 00:22:39.300
includes all the benefit
of Plan 1 and adds support

00:22:39.300 --> 00:22:43.350
for the other Microsoft
Defender for Servers feature,

00:22:43.350 --> 00:22:45.780
so it's the option you choose

00:22:45.780 --> 00:22:48.570
when you need the most
comprehensive server

00:22:48.570 --> 00:22:51.010
protection capabilities.

00:22:53.700 --> 00:22:57.330
Here, we are looking at where
server protection is configured

00:22:57.330 --> 00:23:01.440
through Defender for Cloud
settings and Defender plans.

00:23:01.440 --> 00:23:04.950
This is where we assess
our servers' inventory

00:23:04.950 --> 00:23:09.390
and see what's covered and
where we configure provisioning

00:23:09.390 --> 00:23:13.440
so the right agents and
extension are deployed.

00:23:13.440 --> 00:23:18.120
One important note, if you
turn off autoprovisioning

00:23:18.120 --> 00:23:19.950
of the agent, you will need

00:23:19.950 --> 00:23:23.670
to manually install the
Azure Monitoring Agent

00:23:23.670 --> 00:23:25.890
on your virtual machine.

00:23:25.890 --> 00:23:28.950
This setting can have a
big impact on coverage

00:23:28.950 --> 00:23:32.040
so it's worth validating
during rollout.

00:23:32.040 --> 00:23:37.830
For an application hosted
on Azure App Services,

00:23:37.830 --> 00:23:41.490
Microsoft Defender for App
Service uses the scale

00:23:41.490 --> 00:23:46.980
of the cloud to identify attacks
targeting those applications.

00:23:46.980 --> 00:23:50.730
Attackers often probe
web applications looking

00:23:50.730 --> 00:23:53.400
for weaknesses they can exploit.

00:23:53.400 --> 00:23:57.060
Before requests are routed
to a specific environment,

00:23:57.060 --> 00:24:01.410
traffic to application
running in Azure passes

00:24:01.410 --> 00:24:03.450
through multiple gateways

00:24:03.450 --> 00:24:07.470
where requests are
inspected and logged.

00:24:07.470 --> 00:24:13.950
Defender uses that data to
identify exploits and attackers,

00:24:13.950 --> 00:24:17.100
and to learn new patterns
that can be used

00:24:17.100 --> 00:24:21.060
and applied later to
improve detection.

00:24:21.060 --> 00:24:23.700
When something
suspicious is detected,

00:24:23.700 --> 00:24:26.580
Defender for App Service
generates an alert

00:24:26.580 --> 00:24:30.490
that you can
investigate and remedy.

00:24:32.340 --> 00:24:36.150
Defender for Cloud also provides
cloud workload protection

00:24:36.150 --> 00:24:37.410
for database.

00:24:37.410 --> 00:24:40.920
The idea here is to detect
suspicious behavior

00:24:40.920 --> 00:24:44.130
and attack targeting
database services

00:24:44.130 --> 00:24:49.200
so you can identify threats,
such as abnormal access pattern

00:24:49.200 --> 00:24:52.830
or potential
exploitation attempts.

00:24:52.830 --> 00:24:55.560
For Azure SQL Database
specifically,

00:24:55.560 --> 00:24:59.430
Defender for Cloud provides
a set of alerts designed

00:24:59.430 --> 00:25:02.550
to surface these
database-related threats

00:25:02.550 --> 00:25:06.510
and guide investigation.

00:25:06.510 --> 00:25:10.680
Looking more broadly at database
coverage, Microsoft Defender

00:25:10.680 --> 00:25:16.140
for Database includes threat
protection for Azure Cosmos DB.

00:25:16.140 --> 00:25:18.720
It also provides
threat protection

00:25:18.720 --> 00:25:21.540
for open-source
relational databases,

00:25:21.540 --> 00:25:25.440
including Azure Database for
PostgreS, Azure Database

00:25:25.440 --> 00:25:29.970
for MySQL and Azure
Database for MariahDB.

00:25:29.970 --> 00:25:33.990
For each database type, there
is additional documentation

00:25:33.990 --> 00:25:37.560
available to help you
understand the detection,

00:25:37.560 --> 00:25:42.030
onboarding requirement, and
the investigation workflow.

00:25:42.030 --> 00:25:45.360
And, for open-source
relational databases,

00:25:45.360 --> 00:25:50.550
Defender for Cloud also provides
a defined set of alerts

00:25:50.550 --> 00:25:52.530
that describe what kind

00:25:52.530 --> 00:25:56.740
of suspicious activities
can be detected.

00:25:58.590 --> 00:26:02.340
Storage is another
high-value target.

00:26:02.340 --> 00:26:03.930
And Microsoft Defender

00:26:03.930 --> 00:26:08.700
for Storage provides detection
focused on suspicious access

00:26:08.700 --> 00:26:12.030
or activities involving
storage accounts.

00:26:12.030 --> 00:26:15.810
When Defender detects
potentially malicious behavior,

00:26:15.810 --> 00:26:21.330
such as unusual access
pattern, it generates an alert

00:26:21.330 --> 00:26:25.890
that helps you investigate
what happened and decides

00:26:25.890 --> 00:26:30.370
on remediation steps to
reduce ongoing risks.

00:26:31.740 --> 00:26:34.800
For containerized environments,
Microsoft Defender

00:26:34.800 --> 00:26:37.110
for Container protects
your clusters

00:26:37.110 --> 00:26:39.120
across multiple platforms.

00:26:39.120 --> 00:26:44.190
That includes Azure
Kubernetes Services, or AKS,

00:26:44.190 --> 00:26:49.470
Amazon Elastic Kubernetes
Services, or EKS,

00:26:49.470 --> 00:26:53.040
when it's running in a
connected AWS account,

00:26:53.040 --> 00:26:56.070
and unmanaged
Kubernetes distribution

00:26:56.070 --> 00:27:00.930
when they are connected using
Azure Arc-enabled Kubernetes.

00:27:00.930 --> 00:27:03.180
This coverage is important

00:27:03.180 --> 00:27:07.530
because Kubernetes clusters
can host many workloads

00:27:07.530 --> 00:27:12.090
and identities, and the
ability to detect threats

00:27:12.090 --> 00:27:15.120
and misconfiguration
consistently

00:27:15.120 --> 00:27:19.660
across environment helps
reduce blind spots.

00:27:21.780 --> 00:27:25.350
Microsoft Defender for
Key Vault detects unusual

00:27:25.350 --> 00:27:29.070
and potentially harmful
attempts to access

00:27:29.070 --> 00:27:31.890
or exploit Key Vault accounts.

00:27:31.890 --> 00:27:34.830
This layer of
protection is designed

00:27:34.830 --> 00:27:38.100
to help you address
threats without needing

00:27:38.100 --> 00:27:42.420
to be a security expert and
without having to deploy

00:27:42.420 --> 00:27:46.590
and manage a third-party
security monitoring system.

00:27:46.590 --> 00:27:49.740
When anomalous activities occur,

00:27:49.740 --> 00:27:52.320
Defender for Cloud
shows an alert

00:27:52.320 --> 00:27:55.620
that can optionally
send them by email

00:27:55.620 --> 00:27:59.340
to the relevant people
in your organization.

00:27:59.340 --> 00:28:04.530
These alerts include detail of
the suspicious activities along

00:28:04.530 --> 00:28:07.650
with recommendation
on how to investigate

00:28:07.650 --> 00:28:10.210
and remediate the threats.

00:28:12.060 --> 00:28:16.170
Microsoft Defender for Resource
Manager focuses on threats

00:28:16.170 --> 00:28:21.030
and suspicious activity at
the Azure management layer.

00:28:21.030 --> 00:28:24.540
It helps protect against
suspicious resource management

00:28:24.540 --> 00:28:30.000
operations like actions on
malicious IP addresses,

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:33.000
attempts to disable
anti-malware,

00:28:33.000 --> 00:28:37.800
or suspicious script running
in virtual machine extensions.

00:28:37.800 --> 00:28:41.910
It can also detect the
use of exploitation kits,

00:28:41.910 --> 00:28:46.680
such as a microburst or a
power surge, and, importantly,

00:28:46.680 --> 00:28:51.660
it helps address scenarios
including lateral movement

00:28:51.660 --> 00:28:53.850
from the Azure management layer

00:28:53.850 --> 00:28:57.240
into the Azure
resource data plane

00:28:57.240 --> 00:29:00.240
where attackers may
attempt to pivot

00:29:00.240 --> 00:29:05.080
from control plane access
to workload access.

00:29:06.420 --> 00:29:10.080
Defender for API helps
you gain visibility

00:29:10.080 --> 00:29:14.760
in your business-critical
APIs so you can investigate

00:29:14.760 --> 00:29:20.460
and improve security posture,
prioritize vulnerability fixes,

00:29:20.460 --> 00:29:25.320
and detect against the
top OWASP API threats.

00:29:25.320 --> 00:29:29.940
It provides a unified inventory
of all APIs published

00:29:29.940 --> 00:29:32.760
within Azure API Management.

00:29:32.760 --> 00:29:35.040
It can monitor API traffic

00:29:35.040 --> 00:29:39.540
against top OWASP API threats
using machine learning-based

00:29:39.540 --> 00:29:44.280
detection and threat
intelligence-based detection.

00:29:44.280 --> 00:29:47.340
It also delivers
security insights,

00:29:47.340 --> 00:29:52.800
such as identifying
unauthenticated APIs, inactive

00:29:52.800 --> 00:29:57.390
or dormant APIs, and
externally exposed APIs.

00:29:57.390 --> 00:30:01.140
And it can classify APIs
that receive or respond

00:30:01.140 --> 00:30:06.060
with sensitive data, helping
you focus protection

00:30:06.060 --> 00:30:09.970
where the business impact
is at the highest.

00:30:11.880 --> 00:30:14.370
Workload protection also extends

00:30:14.370 --> 00:30:17.730
into the entire
development pipeline.

00:30:17.730 --> 00:30:22.410
Defender for DevOps provides
a unified visibility

00:30:22.410 --> 00:30:27.180
into the DevOps Security posture
through a central console

00:30:27.180 --> 00:30:31.320
so a security administrator
can see DevOps inventory

00:30:31.320 --> 00:30:33.780
and posture in one place.

00:30:33.780 --> 00:30:37.500
It helps strengthen cloud
resources configuration

00:30:37.500 --> 00:30:41.520
throughout the development
lifecycle by enabling security

00:30:41.520 --> 00:30:46.590
for infrastructure as Code
templates and container images,

00:30:46.590 --> 00:30:47.940
reducing the chance

00:30:47.940 --> 00:30:51.870
that misconfiguration
makes it to production.

00:30:51.870 --> 00:30:56.340
It also helps prioritize
remediation of critical issues

00:30:56.340 --> 00:31:00.090
in Code using Pull
Request annotations

00:31:00.090 --> 00:31:02.910
and assigning
developer ownership

00:31:02.910 --> 00:31:06.390
so fixes can be
tracked and completed.

00:31:06.390 --> 00:31:10.350
And it's worth noting that
DevOps Security is available

00:31:10.350 --> 00:31:14.970
in commercial cloud across
multiple regions including part

00:31:14.970 --> 00:31:22.240
of Asia, Australia, Canada,
United States, UK, and Europe.

00:31:24.540 --> 00:31:27.930
Here, we can see what it
looks like in practice.

00:31:27.930 --> 00:31:31.860
The DevOps Security page
acts as a central console

00:31:31.860 --> 00:31:35.940
where you can view all
onboarding environments along

00:31:35.940 --> 00:31:37.680
with key metrics.

00:31:37.680 --> 00:31:41.220
This gives you an
at-a-glance understanding

00:31:41.220 --> 00:31:47.400
of where coverage exists, which
posture issues are concentrated,

00:31:47.400 --> 00:31:51.630
and which environments may
need immediate attention

00:31:51.630 --> 00:31:55.240
from security and
development team.

00:31:57.240 --> 00:32:01.560
At this point, we have talked
about postures and protections.

00:32:01.560 --> 00:32:04.530
Now we will focus on what
happens when Defender

00:32:04.530 --> 00:32:08.880
for Cloud actually detects
something suspicious.

00:32:08.880 --> 00:32:13.680
Let's walk through how alerts
work, how you respond,

00:32:13.680 --> 00:32:18.660
and how you can automate part
of your remediation workflow.

00:32:18.660 --> 00:32:21.930
In this module, you will
learn how to describe alerts

00:32:21.930 --> 00:32:26.700
in Microsoft Defender for Cloud,
how to respond to those alerts,

00:32:26.700 --> 00:32:30.780
and how to automate response
in Defender for Cloud

00:32:30.780 --> 00:32:35.910
so that common remediation
action can run consistently

00:32:35.910 --> 00:32:37.890
and quickly.

00:32:37.890 --> 00:32:42.090
Here, we focus on what security
alerts really represent

00:32:42.090 --> 00:32:44.460
in Microsoft Defender for Cloud.

00:32:44.460 --> 00:32:48.030
Security alerts and
incidents are generated

00:32:48.030 --> 00:32:53.460
when Defender detects behavior
that may indicate a threat.

00:32:53.460 --> 00:32:57.540
These detections are based
on continuous monitoring

00:32:57.540 --> 00:33:02.190
and ongoing security assessment
across your environment.

00:33:02.190 --> 00:33:05.760
Alerts are classified to help
you understand the nature

00:33:05.760 --> 00:33:10.050
and severity of the issue,
and can represent anything

00:33:10.050 --> 00:33:15.450
from suspicious activity to
confirmed malicious behavior.

00:33:15.450 --> 00:33:17.550
Understanding alert types

00:33:17.550 --> 00:33:20.760
and how they're generated
is the first step

00:33:20.760 --> 00:33:25.300
in responding effectively
and consistently.

00:33:26.520 --> 00:33:29.430
Once an alert is raised,
Microsoft Defender

00:33:29.430 --> 00:33:32.340
for Cloud helps
guide your response.

00:33:32.340 --> 00:33:36.360
Here, we see how alerts
provide actionable tasks

00:33:36.360 --> 00:33:41.040
that helps you mitigate the
threat, prevent similar attacks

00:33:41.040 --> 00:33:45.450
in the future, or trigger
an automated response.

00:33:45.450 --> 00:33:48.810
You can also suppress
alerts when appropriate,

00:33:48.810 --> 00:33:53.250
such as when you've confirmed
an alert is a false positive.

00:33:53.250 --> 00:33:57.870
The goal is to move from
detection to action quickly

00:33:57.870 --> 00:34:02.490
without losing
important context.

00:34:02.490 --> 00:34:06.540
For recurring or
well-understood scenarios,

00:34:06.540 --> 00:34:11.340
automation can significantly
reduce response time.

00:34:11.340 --> 00:34:15.180
Here, we look at how you
can create a logic app

00:34:15.180 --> 00:34:16.860
and define the condition

00:34:16.860 --> 00:34:20.010
under which it should
automatically run.

00:34:20.010 --> 00:34:22.890
By automating alert responses,

00:34:22.890 --> 00:34:27.660
you ensure that remediation
steps are applied consistently

00:34:27.660 --> 00:34:33.520
and immediately, even outside
normal working hours.

00:34:35.190 --> 00:34:39.720
Not every alert requires
action every time.

00:34:39.720 --> 00:34:43.620
Suppression rules are useful
when alerts are identified

00:34:43.620 --> 00:34:47.370
as false positive or when
they trigger too frequently

00:34:47.370 --> 00:34:48.990
to be useful.

00:34:48.990 --> 00:34:52.860
Here, we explore how suppression
rules help reduce noise

00:34:52.860 --> 00:34:56.550
so security teams can
stay focused on alerts

00:34:56.550 --> 00:35:00.690
that truly matter without
losing visibility

00:35:00.690 --> 00:35:03.730
in the overall security health.

00:35:05.220 --> 00:35:07.200
Beyond individual alerts,

00:35:07.200 --> 00:35:10.110
Defender for Cloud
also provides access

00:35:10.110 --> 00:35:12.630
to threat intelligence reports.

00:35:12.630 --> 00:35:15.810
These reports can
include information

00:35:15.810 --> 00:35:20.850
such as activity groups,
campaigns, threat summaries.

00:35:20.850 --> 00:35:25.470
When available, they may also
contain attacker identity

00:35:25.470 --> 00:35:30.270
or association, attacker
objectives, and current

00:35:30.270 --> 00:35:33.090
or historical attack campaigns.

00:35:33.090 --> 00:35:37.290
You will also find details
against attackers' tactics,

00:35:37.290 --> 00:35:41.550
tools, and procedures,
associated indicators

00:35:41.550 --> 00:35:47.400
of compromise like URLs or
file hashes, victimology data,

00:35:47.400 --> 00:35:51.450
and guidance on mitigation
and remediation.

00:35:51.450 --> 00:35:55.710
This broader context helps
you understand not just what

00:35:55.710 --> 00:36:00.030
happened, but why it matters.

00:36:00.030 --> 00:36:04.470
Here, we bring everything
together with a focus

00:36:04.470 --> 00:36:09.270
on responding to alerts from
specific Azure resources.

00:36:09.270 --> 00:36:13.170
This includes responding
to Azure Defender

00:36:13.170 --> 00:36:18.810
for Key Vault alerts, DNS
alerts, resource manager alerts.

00:36:18.810 --> 00:36:23.100
This is also where we
manage our alerts,

00:36:23.100 --> 00:36:28.750
review the alert details,
and configure automation.

00:36:31.110 --> 00:36:33.990
At this point, we will
move into a demonstration

00:36:33.990 --> 00:36:37.830
that ties together the concept
from this Learning Path.

00:36:37.830 --> 00:36:42.510
Here, we will see how Microsoft
Defender for Cloud is used end

00:36:42.510 --> 00:36:46.320
to end to mitigate
threats from detection

00:36:46.320 --> 00:36:50.410
through investigation
and response.

00:36:51.870 --> 00:36:53.310
In this demonstration,

00:36:53.310 --> 00:36:56.700
let's enable Microsoft
Defender for Cloud.

00:36:56.700 --> 00:36:59.880
We have got the Azure Portal
open so let's navigate

00:36:59.880 --> 00:37:01.970
to Defender for Cloud.

00:37:01.970 --> 00:37:04.430
We'll just search for
it in the Search bar.

00:37:04.430 --> 00:37:08.190
There is Microsoft
Defender for Cloud.

00:37:08.190 --> 00:37:11.040
What we need to do now
is enable Defender

00:37:11.040 --> 00:37:13.710
for Cloud for our subscription.

00:37:13.710 --> 00:37:16.290
So let's head down to
Environment Settings

00:37:16.290 --> 00:37:18.990
under the Management section.

00:37:18.990 --> 00:37:23.310
Here is our management group
including our subscription.

00:37:23.310 --> 00:37:26.940
We'll just expand everything,
select a subscription

00:37:26.940 --> 00:37:30.820
where we want to enable
Defender for Cloud.

00:37:32.430 --> 00:37:37.680
Within the subscription, we
can enable individual plan,

00:37:37.680 --> 00:37:42.540
like Defender CSPM, foundational
is always turned on.

00:37:42.540 --> 00:37:47.130
But let's enable Defender Cloud
Security Posture Management.

00:37:47.130 --> 00:37:50.630
Done. Let's do the
same with our server.

00:37:50.630 --> 00:37:53.300
Let's activate Plan 2.

00:37:53.300 --> 00:37:57.530
On. Done. And we could
activate additional plans

00:37:57.530 --> 00:37:59.750
if we wanted to for Defender.

00:37:59.750 --> 00:38:03.180
But, for now, let's just save.

00:38:03.180 --> 00:38:04.260
And we are good.

00:38:04.260 --> 00:38:07.600
We have activated
Defender for Cloud.

00:38:08.940 --> 00:38:11.790
We could also click
the "Enable All Plans"

00:38:11.790 --> 00:38:13.410
to turn on everything.

00:38:13.410 --> 00:38:16.200
Let's go back to
Defender for Cloud

00:38:16.200 --> 00:38:21.630
and let's now explore some
useful capability like the

00:38:21.630 --> 00:38:24.900
"What's New" button,
keep yourself up to date

00:38:24.900 --> 00:38:26.340
with Defender for Cloud.

00:38:26.340 --> 00:38:29.010
It evolves very quickly.

00:38:29.010 --> 00:38:35.310
Next, let's review our
regulatory compliance.

00:38:35.310 --> 00:38:38.130
Actually, we don't have any
so let's activate those,

00:38:38.130 --> 00:38:42.450
Manage Regulatory Compliances.

00:38:42.450 --> 00:38:44.910
From here, once again,
we'll select where we want

00:38:44.910 --> 00:38:47.070
to activate that compliance.

00:38:47.070 --> 00:38:49.680
We'll select "Our Subscription."

00:38:49.680 --> 00:38:56.490
And, under Security Policies,
here is a list of compliances

00:38:56.490 --> 00:38:59.580
that are already built
for you by Microsoft.

00:38:59.580 --> 00:39:02.310
They're included in
Defender for Cloud.

00:39:02.310 --> 00:39:08.630
Let's select "ISO 27001-2013."

00:39:08.630 --> 00:39:11.720
And let's just enable it.

00:39:11.720 --> 00:39:14.690
Done. We are good.

00:39:14.690 --> 00:39:18.260
Now that we've assigned a
standard, let's take a look

00:39:18.260 --> 00:39:23.130
at the recommendations
brought on by this standard.

00:39:23.130 --> 00:39:26.100
We're going to head back
to Defender for Cloud,

00:39:26.100 --> 00:39:28.320
just close our
Environmental Settings,

00:39:28.320 --> 00:39:32.250
Regulatory Compliance,
back to the overview.

00:39:32.250 --> 00:39:33.990
There is our recommendation.

00:39:33.990 --> 00:39:36.120
Now, we're not going
to see recommendations

00:39:36.120 --> 00:39:40.170
yet because we don't really
have resources onboarded,

00:39:40.170 --> 00:39:44.730
and also the policy is
being applied right now.

00:39:44.730 --> 00:39:49.140
But this is where we would see
the recommendations by Microsoft

00:39:49.140 --> 00:39:53.410
for our Cloud Security
Posture Management.

00:39:55.310 --> 00:39:57.740
Let's take a moment to
recap what we've covered

00:39:57.740 --> 00:39:59.580
in this Learning Path.

00:39:59.580 --> 00:40:03.030
We started by looking at
Microsoft Defender for Cloud

00:40:03.030 --> 00:40:08.190
as a DevSecOps solution that
unifies security management

00:40:08.190 --> 00:40:12.780
across multi-cloud and
multiple-pipeline environments.

00:40:12.780 --> 00:40:16.410
We explored how it delivered
Cloud Security Posture

00:40:16.410 --> 00:40:19.770
Management and cloud
workload protection;

00:40:19.770 --> 00:40:24.420
how it protects workloads like
servers, containers, storage,

00:40:24.420 --> 00:40:28.110
and databases; and how
it extends protection

00:40:28.110 --> 00:40:35.840
to hybrid environments including
non-Azure machines, AWS and GCP.

00:40:35.840 --> 00:40:37.970
Finally, we looked
at how Defender

00:40:37.970 --> 00:40:42.650
for Cloud helps you remediate
alerts with actionable guidance

00:40:42.650 --> 00:40:45.840
and automated responses.

00:40:46.980 --> 00:40:49.110
This concludes our session.

00:40:49.110 --> 00:40:52.830
There are many ways to continue
your learning journey.

